sec.p:
The dynamics of gene product concentrations in each cell in our model obey the following system of Ng reaction-diffusion equations (Murray 2002),
(1)
∂tg(t,x)=f(g(t,x))−Mgm(t,x)+D∇x2g(t,x),for allt>0and allx \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \boldsymbol{x}$$\end{document}
With initial pattern (i.e. initial condition),
(2)
g(0,x)=g0(x), for allx \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \boldsymbol{x}$$\end{document}
And boundary conditions,
(3)
n^∇xg(t,xb)=0, for allt>0and all boundaryxb, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \boldsymbol{x}_{\boldsymbol{b}}$$\end{document}
where ∂t is the time derivative ; x is space in (n=1,2,3 dimensions) ; g(t,x)=(g1(t,x),…,gNg(t,x))∈R≥0Ng\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{g}(t, \boldsymbol{x})=\left(g_{1}(t, \boldsymbol{x}), \ldots, g_{N_{g}}(t, \boldsymbol{x})\right) \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{N_{g}}$\end{document} is the vector of gene product concentrations (from gene 1 to Ng) at the cell at time t and position x. gm(t,x)=(g1m1(t,x),…,gNgmNg(t,x))∈R≥0Ng\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{g}^{\boldsymbol{m}}(t, \boldsymbol{x})=\left(g_{1}^{m_{1}}(t, \boldsymbol{x}), \ldots, g_{N_{g}}^{m_{N_{g}}}(t, \boldsymbol{x})\right) \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^{N_{g}}$\end{document} is a vector of powers of the gene product concentrations and m is a vector of positive integer exponents; f=(f1,…,fNg):RNg→RNg\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{f}=\left(f_{1}, \ldots, f_{N_{g}}\right): \mathbb{R}^{N_{g}} \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^{N_{g}}$\end{document} governs the interactions between gene products and determines how much of each gene product i is being synthesized in a given instant of time. f is, thus, a function that takes a vector of concentrations as input and returns another vector of concentrations as output ; ∇2 denotes the Laplace operator (i.e., ∂xx2g(t,x)\begin{document}$\partial_{x x}^{2} \boldsymbol{g}(t, \boldsymbol{x})$\end{document} in (∂xx2+∂yy2)g(t,x)\begin{document}$\left(\partial_{x x}^{2}+\partial_{y y}^{2}\right) \boldsymbol{g}(t, \boldsymbol{x})$\end{document} in 2D; in (∂x2+∂y2+∂z2)g(t,x)\begin{document}$\left(\partial_{x}^{2}+\partial_{y}^{2}+\partial_{z}^{2}\right) \boldsymbol{g}(t, \boldsymbol{x})$\end{document} in 3D). D=diag(d1,…,dNg)\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{D}=\operatorname{diag}\left(d_{1}, \ldots, d_{N_{g}}\right)$\end{document} is an Ng × Ng -diagonal matrix with real positive diffusion coefficients for each gene product along its diagonal. By definition extracellular signals have di>0 while for intracellular gene products di=0. Similarly, M=diag(μ1,…,μNg)\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{M}=\operatorname{diag}\left(\mu_{1}, \ldots, \mu_{N_{g}}\right)$\end{document} is a Ng × Ng-diagonal matrix with the real degradation coefficients for each gene product along its diagonal, μi≥0. The degradation coefficient of a gene product is the default rate at which it would be degraded by proteases if no other factors affect its degradation. Notice that equation (1) has Ng components and applies to each cell. For the cases of extracellular signals, i.e. their concentration in a cell can affect that in distant cells through the diffusion term (last term in (1)).

To analyze whether a perturbation will grow we will use the standard linear stability analysis of the dynamical system defined by our model equations (1)-(3). In this study we focus in those f functions for which equation (1) to (3) admit a steady state homogeneous solution g(t, x) ≡ g* (i.e. a homogeneous steady state) given by f (g*)− M (g*)m=0. Hence, let g~(t,x)=g(t,x)−g∗\begin{document}$\widetilde{\boldsymbol{g}}(t, \boldsymbol{x})=\boldsymbol{g}(t, \boldsymbol{x})-\boldsymbol{g}^{*}$\end{document} denote a small concentration perturbation from the homogeneous steady state g*. Then, plugging g~(t,x)\begin{document}$\widetilde{\boldsymbol{g}}(t, \boldsymbol{x})$\end{document} back into (1)-(3) shows that the evolution of such perturbation in space and time is given by the linearized reaction-diffusion equations,

(5)

{∂tg~(t,x)=R(0)g~(t,x)+D∇2g~(t,x),for allt>0and allx;g~(0,x)=g~0(x),for allx;n^∇g~(t,xb)=0,for allt>0and all boundaryxb;
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{cases}\partial_{t} \tilde{\boldsymbol{g}}(t, \boldsymbol{x})=\boldsymbol{R}(0) \tilde{\boldsymbol{g}}(t, \boldsymbol{x})+\boldsymbol{D} \nabla^{2} \tilde{\boldsymbol{g}}(t, \boldsymbol{x}), & \text { for all } t>0 \text { and all } \boldsymbol{x} ;  \tag{5}\\ \tilde{\boldsymbol{g}}(0, \boldsymbol{x})=\tilde{\boldsymbol{g}}_{\mathbf{0}}(\boldsymbol{x}), & \text { for all } \boldsymbol{x} ; \\ \hat{\boldsymbol{n}} \nabla \tilde{\boldsymbol{g}}\left(t, \boldsymbol{x}_{\boldsymbol{b}}\right)=0, & \text { for all } t>0 \text { and all boundary } \boldsymbol{x}_{\boldsymbol{b}} ;\end{cases}$$\end{document}



where R(0)=Jf∗−m⊙M(g∗)m−1\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{R}(0)=\boldsymbol{J}_{f}^{*}-\boldsymbol{m} \odot \boldsymbol{M}\left(\boldsymbol{g}^{*}\right)^{m-1}$\end{document} is the reaction-diffusion matrix in the absence of diffusion; and g~0(x)=g0(x)−g∗\begin{document}$\widetilde{\boldsymbol{g}}_{\mathbf{0}}(\boldsymbol{x})=\boldsymbol{g}_{0}(\boldsymbol{x})-\boldsymbol{g}^{*}$\end{document} represents the initial perturbation. In the reaction diffusion matrix, Jf∗=Jf(g∗)\begin{document}$J_{f}^{*}=J_{f}\left(g^{*}\right)$\end{document} is the Jacobian matrix of f (i.e. the matrix of derivatives of f (g) with respect to each gene product) evaluated at the steady state g*, and u⊙v=(u1v1,…,uNqvNq)\begin{document}$\boldsymbol{u} \odot \boldsymbol{v}=\left(u_{1} v_{1}, \ldots, u_{N_{q}} v_{N_{q}}\right)$\end{document} denotes component-by-component vector multiplication.

Eigenmodes Wk(x) can be seen as a generalization of classical Fourier modes and hence, we can now consider the series expansion of g~(t,x)\begin{document}$\widetilde{\boldsymbol{g}}(t, \boldsymbol{x})$\end{document} in terms of Wk(x), that is,
(7)
g~(t,x)=∑k∈σ(∇2)c^k(t)Wk(x), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \widetilde{\boldsymbol{g}}(t, \boldsymbol{x})=\sum_{\boldsymbol{k} \in \sigma\left(\nabla^{2}\right)} \hat{\boldsymbol{c}}_{\boldsymbol{k}}(t) W_{\boldsymbol{k}}(\boldsymbol{x}), \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where each c^k(t)∈RNg\begin{document}$\hat{\boldsymbol{c}}_{\boldsymbol{k}}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^{N_{g}}$\end{document} is given by,
(8)
c^k(t)=∫g(t,x)Wk(x)dx. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{\boldsymbol{c}}_{\boldsymbol{k}}(t)=\int \boldsymbol{\boldsymbol { g }}(t, \boldsymbol{x}) W_{\boldsymbol{k}}(\boldsymbol{x}) \mathrm{d} \boldsymbol{x} . \tag{8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
If we now plug series expansion (7) into system (5) and use the linearity of the equations to split the sum into terms for each k, we get,
(9)
∂tc^k(t)Wk(x)=R(0)c^k(t)Wk(x)+Dc^k(t)∇2Wk(x); \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \partial_{t} \hat{\boldsymbol{c}}_{\boldsymbol{k}}(t) W_{\boldsymbol{k}}(\boldsymbol{x})=\boldsymbol{R}(0) \hat{\boldsymbol{c}}_{\boldsymbol{k}}(t) W_{\boldsymbol{k}}(\boldsymbol{x})+\boldsymbol{D} \hat{\boldsymbol{c}}_{\boldsymbol{k}}(t) \nabla^{2} W_{\boldsymbol{k}}(\boldsymbol{x}) ;$$\end{document}
and then, if we use (6) to solve the space derivatives, and eliminate alike terms from each side of the equations (i.e., Wk(x)), we get that the time evolution of each ĉk(t) is given by the equation,
(10)
∂tc^k(t)=R(k2)c^k(t), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \partial_{t} \hat{\boldsymbol{c}}_{\boldsymbol{k}}(t)=\boldsymbol{R}\left(\boldsymbol{k}^{2}\right) \hat{\boldsymbol{c}}_{\boldsymbol{k}}(t), \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where R(k2)=R(0)+k2D=Jf(g*)−m⊙M(g*)m−1 is the so-called reaction-diffusion matrix. Solutions to equation (10) are given by,
(11)
c^k(t)=exp⁡(R(k2)t)c^k(0), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{\boldsymbol{c}}_{\boldsymbol{k}}(t)=\exp \left(\boldsymbol{R}\left(\boldsymbol{k}^{2}\right) t\right) \hat{\boldsymbol{c}}_{\boldsymbol{k}}^{(0)}, \tag{11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where exp(R(k2)t) is a matrix exponential; and each c^k(0)=c^k(0)∈RNg\begin{document}$\hat{\boldsymbol{c}}_{\boldsymbol{k}}^{(0)}=\hat{\boldsymbol{c}}_{\boldsymbol{k}}(0) \in \mathbb{R}^{N_{g}}$\end{document} is computed as in (8), changing g~(t,x)\begin{document}$\widetilde{\boldsymbol{g}}(t, \boldsymbol{x})$\end{document} by the corresponding initial perturbation g~0(x)\begin{document}$\widetilde{\boldsymbol{g}}_{0}(x)$\end{document}.

Let us now consider the case of a gene network with only one loop, which we assume to contain Nl>1 gene products. Without loss of generality, gene products can be relabeled so that the submatrix of λINg−R(k2)\begin{document}$\lambda \boldsymbol{I}_{N_{g}}-\boldsymbol{R}\left(\boldsymbol{k}^{2}\right)$\end{document} of the loop contains all non-zero elements under the diagonal and in the top-right entry. Namely,
(16)
(λ+μ1+k2d100…0−J1Nl−J21λ+μ2+k2d20…000−J31λ+μ3+k2d3…00⋮⋮⋮⋱⋮⋮000…λ+μNl−1+k2dNl−10000…−J(Nl−1)Nlλ+μNl+k2dNl). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \left(\begin{array}{cccccc} \lambda+\mu_{1}+\boldsymbol{k}^{2} d_{1} & 0 & 0 & \ldots & 0 & -J_{1 N_{l}} \tag{16}\\ -J_{21} & \lambda+\mu_{2}+\boldsymbol{k}^{2} d_{2} & 0 & \ldots & 0 & 0 \\ 0 & -J_{31} & \lambda+\mu_{3}+\boldsymbol{k}^{2} d_{3} & \ldots & 0 & 0 \\ \vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots & \vdots \\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \ldots & \lambda+\mu_{N_{l}-1}+\boldsymbol{k}^{2} d_{N_{l}-1} & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & 0 & \ldots & -J_{\left(N_{l}-1\right) N_{l}} & \lambda+\mu_{N_{l}}+\boldsymbol{k}^{2} d_{N_{l}} \end{array}\right) .$$\end{document}
Similarly, given that there exists no other loop in the network, we can relabel the remaining gene products so that the rest of the matrix is upper triangular (Bang-Jensen, 2008). Thus, the characteristic polynomial has two terms: the product of all diagonal entries, and the product of the non-diagonal entries in the loop times the remaining Ng−Nl diagonal entries. The corresponding dispersion relation reads,
(17)
det(λINg−R(k2))=∏i=1Ng(λ+μi+k2di)+(−1)Nl+1(−J1Ng)∏i=2Nl−1(−Ji(i−1))∏i=1Ng−Nl(λ+μi+k2di)=0, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{align*} & \operatorname{det}\left(\lambda \boldsymbol{I}_{N_{g}}-\boldsymbol{R}\left(\boldsymbol{k}^{2}\right)\right)= \\ & \quad \prod_{i=1}^{N_{g}}\left(\lambda+\mu_{i}+\boldsymbol{k}^{2} d_{i}\right)+(-1)^{N_{l}+1}\left(-J_{1 N_{g}}\right) \prod_{i=2}^{N_{l}-1}\left(-J_{i(i-1)}\right) \prod_{i=1}^{N_{g}-N_{l}}\left(\lambda+\mu_{i}+\boldsymbol{k}^{2} d_{i}\right)=0, \tag{17} \end{align*}$$\end{document}
where the minus sign before each Jij comes from (13) and the factor (−1)Ng+1\begin{document}$(-1)^{N_{g}+1}$\end{document} comes from the Leibniz formula for the case of a loop like (16). A (−1) factor can be extracted from each Jacobian entry to obtain a (−1)Nl\begin{document}$(-1)^{N_{l}}$\end{document} factor that then simplifies (17) to,
(18)
det(λINg−R(k2))=∏i=1Ng(λ+μi+k2di)−J(1,Ng)∏i=2N1−1J(i,i−1)∏i=1Ng−N1(λ+μi+k2di)=0. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \operatorname{det}\left(\lambda \boldsymbol{I}_{N_{g}}-\boldsymbol{R}\left(\boldsymbol{k}^{2}\right)\right)=\prod_{i=1}^{N_{g}}\left(\lambda+\mu_{i}+\boldsymbol{k}^{2} d_{i}\right)-J_{\left(1, N_{g}\right)} \prod_{i=2}^{N_{1}-1} J_{(i, i-1)} \prod_{i=1}^{N_{g}-N_{1}}\left(\lambda+\mu_{i}+\boldsymbol{k}^{2} d_{i}\right)=0 . \tag{18} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Similarly, the factor (λ+μi+k2di) is found Ng times in the left term and Ng−Nl times in the right term and so, (18) can be further simplified into,
(19)
det(λINg−R(k2))=∏i=1N1(λ+μi+k2di)−J(1,Ng)∏i=2N1−1J(i,i−1)=0. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \operatorname{det}\left(\lambda \boldsymbol{I}_{N_{g}}-\boldsymbol{R}\left(\boldsymbol{k}^{2}\right)\right)=\prod_{i=1}^{N_{1}}\left(\lambda+\mu_{i}+\boldsymbol{k}^{2} d_{i}\right)-J_{\left(1, N_{g}\right)} \prod_{i=2}^{N_{1}-1} J_{(i, i-1)}=0 . \tag{19} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
All terms in the first product of (19) are positive and, thus, its expansion is an Ng-polynomial in λ with positive coefficients,
(20)
det(λINg−R(k2))=λNg+…+∏i=1Nl(μi+k2di)−J(1,Ng)∏i=2NlJ(i,i−1)=0; \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \operatorname{det}\left(\lambda \boldsymbol{I}_{N_{g}}-\boldsymbol{R}\left(\boldsymbol{k}^{2}\right)\right)=\lambda^{N_{g}}+\ldots+\prod_{i=1}^{N_{l}}\left(\mu_{i}+\boldsymbol{k}^{2} d_{i}\right)-J_{\left(1, N_{g}\right)} \prod_{i=2}^{N_{l}} J_{(i, i-1)}=0 ; \tag{20} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
and then, the characteristic polynomial has a negative constant term if:
(21)
J(1,Ng)∏i=2NlJ(i,i−1)>∏i=1Nl(μi+k2di). \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} J_{\left(1, N_{g}\right)} \prod_{i=2}^{N_{l}} J_{(i, i-1)}>\prod_{i=1}^{N_{l}}\left(\mu_{i}+\boldsymbol{k}^{2} d_{i}\right) . \tag{21} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Notice condition (21) only holds if the regulatory loop is positive (i.e. all regulations are positive or there is an even number of negative ones). If condition (21) holds for some k, it follows that the characteristic polynomial takes a negative value for λ=0. Moreover, since the term with the largest degree is positive, the characteristic polynomial becomes positive as λ →∞. This implies that the polynomial has to cross the λ-axis at least at one positive λ and such positive root is a positive eigenvalue of R(k2) for any k satisfying (21). In other words, we have proven that the dispersion relation of the gene network with a positive regulatory loop can yield unstable wavenumbers and, thus, the network can be RD-unstable depending on the parameters.

To illustrate these arguments lets us consider an example gene network and f in which signal A directly activates gene products j and k, and k directly inhibits j in such a way that j and k are regulated by no other gene product in the network. In H0 networks the concentration of A is given by equation (23) and the dynamics of j and k are given by (24).
(24)
{∂tgk(t,x)=fk(gA(t,x))−μkgk(t,x)∂tgj(t,x)=fj(gA(t,x),gk(t,x))−μjgj(t,x) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \left\{\begin{array}{l} \partial_{t} g_{k}(t, x)=f_{k}\left(g_{A}(t, x)\right)-\mu_{k} g_{k}(t, x) \tag{24}\\ \partial_{t} g_{j}(t, x)=f_{j}\left(g_{A}(t, x), g_{k}(t, x)\right)-\mu_{j} g_{j}(t, x) \end{array}\right.$$\end{document}
Where we assume, for simplicity, that both j and k suffer linear degradation (i.e., mj=mk=1). Now, assume that regulations fk(gA) and fj(gA, gk) are both linear. Then, system (24) reads,
(25)
{∂tgk(t,x)=BkAgA(t,x)−μkgk(t,x),∂tgj(t,x)=BjAgA(t,x)−Bjkgk(t,x)−μjgj(t,x), \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \left\{\begin{array}{l} \partial_{t} g_{k}(t, x)=B_{k A} g_{A}(t, x)-\mu_{k} g_{k}(t, x), \tag{25}\\ \partial_{t} g_{j}(t, x)=B_{j A} g_{A}(t, x)-B_{j k} g_{k}(t, x)-\mu_{j} g_{j}(t, x), \end{array}\right.$$\end{document}
where BkA, BjA, Bjk >0 are real positive constants. Given that we are only interested in stationary resulting patterns, we can take time derivatives in (25) to zero and then we find that the concentrations of k and j in the resulting pattern are given by,
(26)
gk∗(x)=BkAμkCe−μAdAx,andgj∗(x)=(BjAμj−BkABjkμkμj)Ce−μAdAx, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} g_{k}^{*}(x)=\frac{B_{k A}}{\mu_{k}} C e^{-\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{A}}{d_{A}}} x}, \text { and } g_{j}^{*}(x)=\left(\frac{B_{j A}}{\mu_{j}}-\frac{B_{k A} B_{j k}}{\mu_{k} \mu_{j}}\right) C e^{-\sqrt{\frac{\mu_{A}}{d_{A}}} x}, \tag{26} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where we have used the gradient shape of gA∗(x)\begin{document}$g_{A}^{*}(x)$\end{document} from (23). Then, as we can see in (26), both concentrations have negative exponential distributions in space with the same exponential decay but different heights (i.e., different leading coefficients). Moreover, equation (26) also shows that there is a single concentration peak for A, j and k, and that these peaks are all centered at the same position (i.e. the center of the spike) and, thus, there is no non-trivial pattern transformation. This proves that no non-trivial pattern transformations are possible if all regulations are linear (requirement R2).
